ARTICLE = '''<p class="lead">Ask five exhibitors what their stand cost and you will get five very different answers. That is not because anyone is guessing. An exhibition stand is priced like a small building project: by footprint, by complexity and by where it is being built. This guide walks through realistic 2026 ranges and, more usefully, what sits behind them.</p>
<h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
<p>A 10 &times; 10 ft rental inline stand starts around $4,200. A custom 20 &times; 20 ft island typically lands between $58,000 and $92,000. Double-decker stands and multi-exhibitor pavilions start above $150,000. These are demonstration ranges for structure and design; graphics, lighting, furniture and installation are extra, and your own quote depends on the factors below.</p>
<h2 id="cost-drivers">The main cost drivers</h2>
<ul><li><strong>Booth size.</strong> The biggest single factor, though cost per square foot usually falls as stands get larger.</li><li><strong>Design complexity.</strong> Curves, double-height walls and hanging structures need more design and fabrication hours.</li><li><strong>Materials.</strong> Timber, aluminium, solid surface and glass all carry different costs and lifespans.</li><li><strong>Graphics.</strong> Fabric and backlit graphics cost more than direct-print panels.</li><li><strong>Lighting and technology.</strong> Feature lighting, LED walls and interactive demos add hardware, programming and power.</li><li><strong>Installation and location.</strong> Venue labour rules, freight and the number of install days vary widely between cities.</li></ul>
<blockquote class="pullquote"><p>Simplify the structure before you simplify the graphics. Walls are expensive; a great message on a simple wall is not.</p></blockquote>
<h2 id="price-ranges">Typical price ranges by booth</h2>
<div class="table-wrap" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Typical price ranges"><table class="table table--stack"><caption class="sr-only">Typical price ranges by booth size</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Booth</th><th scope="col">Rental (per show)</th><th scope="col">Custom build</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><th scope="row" data-label="Booth">10 &times; 10 inline</th><td data-label="Rental (per show)">$4,200 - $7,200</td><td data-label="Custom build">$9,500 - $15,000</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row" data-label="Booth">10 &times; 20 inline</th><td data-label="Rental (per show)">$8,400 - $14,400</td><td data-label="Custom build">$19,000 - $30,000</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row" data-label="Booth">20 &times; 20 island</th><td data-label="Rental (per show)">$22,000 - $34,000</td><td data-label="Custom build">$58,000 - $92,000</td></tr>
<tr><th scope="row" data-label="Booth">30 &times; 30 island</th><td data-label="Rental (per show)">$49,500 - $76,500</td><td data-label="Custom build">$130,500 - $207,000</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<h2 id="custom-vs-rental">Custom or rental: what changes</h2>
<p>A rental stand is built from an engineered kit, so you are paying for the days you use it, not for a structure you will own. A custom stand costs more up front but can be stored and reconfigured, which typically lowers the cost per show after the second or third use. Many exhibitors now combine the two: a rental structure with a custom counter and fascia.</p>
<h2 id="hidden-costs">Costs outside the stand</h2>
<p>Hall-controlled services such as electricity, rigging, drayage, cleaning and internet are billed separately by the organiser and often add 15 to 25 percent to the total show cost. Freight to and from the venue, travel for your team and giveaways sit outside the stand budget too. Ask for the organiser&rsquo;s price list early.</p>
<h2 id="save">Where you can save without losing impact</h2>
<ul><li>Lock the design early to avoid rush fees.</li><li>Reuse structural elements across shows and change only the graphics.</li><li>Put money into two or three hero moments, not everywhere.</li><li>Choose one strong lighting feature instead of several weak ones.</li></ul>
<h2 id="quote">How to get an accurate quote</h2>
<p>Send your designer the show name and hall, the footprint and open sides, your goals, the products you need to show and your budget ceiling. With those five things you should receive a layout idea, a fixed-scope price and a schedule within about a week. You can also try our <a href="pricing.html#estimator">project estimator</a> for a quick ballpark.</p>'''


